    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d246">

                <pb facs="0275" n="271" xml:id="pg271"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00146/724</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Statement by the Department of State</hi><anchor n="64"
                        xml:id="d246-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1939-09-27">undated</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>The U. S. expects not to withdraw its landed armed forces from the points at
                    which they are now stationed.</p>
                <p>We note that as of August 4 the British force at Tientsin numbered 494 and at
                    Peiping 216. At that time the U. S. force at Tientsin numbered 226 and at
                    Peiping 344. It appears that subsequently the British have reduced their force
                    at Peiping by about 100 and that the French have done likewise. We assume that
                    these removals from Peiping have been made for purposes of reinforcement at
                    Tientsin. Also, this indicates to us that the British and the French Governments
                    have thus far felt, as we continue to feel, that there are good reasons for
                    keeping the flag flying at Peiping even though the number of men there be
                    reduced.</p>
                <p>It appears that the Italians have been making shifts of personnel from Peiping to
                    Tientsin.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d246-f1" n="64" type="source" xml:id="d246-f1-content">
